On Fri, 2026-05-22 at 15:48 +0000, sashiko-bot@kernel.org wrote:
>=20
> [ ... ]
>=20
> > +	spin_lock(&ctx->flc_lock);
> > +	list_for_each_entry(flc, &ctx->flc_lease, flc_list) {
> > +		struct file_lease *fl =3D container_of(flc, struct file_lease, c);
> > +		struct nfs4_delegation *dp =3D flc->flc_owner;
>=20
> [Severity: Critical]
> Is it safe to assume the lease is owned by NFSD here? Third-party leases
> like local POSIX leases could exist on the directory, meaning their fl_ow=
ner
> has a different type. Should this verify that
> fl->fl_lmops =3D=3D &nfsd_lease_mng_ops before casting to a nfs4_delegati=
on
> pointer to avoid wild pointer dereferences?
>=20
> > +		struct nfsd4_cb_notify *ncn =3D &dp->dl_cb_notify;
> > +
> > +		if (!should_notify_deleg(mask, fl))
> > +			continue;

Sashiko seems to flag this sort of thing a lot. We do sometimes set
pointers to things before we know whether they will be valid.

In this case, the pointers won't be used if fl->fl_lmops !=3D
&nfsd_lease_mng_ops, because should_notify_deleg() will return false in
that case. The compiler will usually not bother doing any of the work
to get those pointers.

Is it wrong to depend on that?
--=20
